<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shaktism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shakta, Shaktas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shaktism is a major tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> that worships the goddess, Devi or Shakti, as the supreme power of the universe. Based on texts such as the Devi Mahatmya and the Tantras, it reveres goddesses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>, Kali and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakhya-temple">Kamakhya</a>. It has been especially strong in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, where many medieval kings were patrons of goddess temples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shaktism.jpg" alt="Shaktism" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nomu420 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statues_of_Vaishnavi,_Varahi,_Indrani_and_Camunda,_National_Museum,_New_Delhi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
